WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.490
The film touches on so many
different themes, one that really stood

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:07.529
out to me was the kind of immense inner
strength that women have - is that quite

00:00:07.529 --> 00:00:11.400
important to you when you're taking on a
role, to portray that? I mean I'm always

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:15.719
going to be more attracted to women who
have some kind of conflict to play and

00:00:15.719 --> 00:00:19.380
something that's not just a
two-dimensional character so yeah that's

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:25.590
always - the draw is to play a character
with depth and and a sense of meaning

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:30.539
and not just to sort of reactionary role
to a male co-star you know. But I think

00:00:30.539 --> 00:00:35.160
strength is not necessarily the word
that I look for, I look for complexity

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:38.910
and because you know it's that funny
thing that no one ever describes a male

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:42.329
character is like 'wow that's a really
strong character', it's sort of, and I feel

00:00:42.329 --> 00:00:46.289
like the same should go for female roles
as well we should just accept people as

00:00:46.289 --> 00:00:50.670
being complicated and flawed and you
know everything that people, that women

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:54.329
are, you know. So one of the most tense
and emotional moments in the film is the

00:00:54.329 --> 00:00:59.399
bathtub scene. I was writhing in my seat,
because it was so stressful.

00:00:59.399 --> 00:01:04.589
Obviously labour is such a difficult
thing and with the added circumstances it

00:01:04.589 --> 00:01:09.060
must have been quite a stressful
headspace to get in to. Yeah I mean I think it

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:13.380
was quite a stressful headspace, I mean we
shot it over five days that, from start

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:16.049
to finish, from the moment the water
breaks till the moment that

00:01:16.049 --> 00:01:19.710
had you squirming, in the bathtub. I feel like
everyone on set was like crossing their

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:26.159
legs... Yes! But it was an exhausting sequence I
will say that, but ultimately I knew

00:01:26.159 --> 00:01:29.579
that it was gonna pack a
punch, I think you're a little excited by

00:01:29.579 --> 00:01:33.329
doing those sequences because you know
what it'll mean to an audience. Yeah. So

00:01:33.329 --> 00:01:35.430
you and John I've obviously worked
together quite a few times and you're

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:41.970
married... Well not really worked together....A few - the voiceovers. So with this film the dialogue

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:45.060
was quite different as in, there wasn't very much of it, did you find that you

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:47.700
learnt anything about each other while
you were working together because there was

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:51.810
less talking going on? I mean the only
thing I can think of is that in some

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:57.450
ways you are a slightly different person
when you're at work and and I think that

00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:00.899
that was the thing we were a bit nervous
about is whether we'd be able to embrace

00:02:00.899 --> 00:02:05.939
that about the person you know that this
is not domestic life when you're on set.

00:02:05.939 --> 00:02:11.099
And I think we, the only things that
surprised us was sort of just how well

00:02:11.099 --> 00:02:15.630
we collaborated together and I think
everything else when it came to his

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:20.070
ability and his talent as a director and
as a filmmaker and a visionary person

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:24.600
was something I've always known about
him and I was just more excited for

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:28.560
people to find that out too. Your career
has gone from strength to strength which

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:31.110
obviously means a lot of travelling, a lot
of late nights, a lot of early

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:34.650
mornings. Have you got any wellness hacks
that you kind of picked up along the

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:38.220
road that you want to share with our
followers? I would say if you feel

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:41.190
yourself coming down with a cold which
I'm like the worst person to nominate

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:47.520
this because I sound horrible today, tail
end of laryngitis, but but I do find oil

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:52.560
of oregano... Oh... Oil of oregano. Oh I sound, I'm so used to hearing the American version. Oil

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:59.550
of oregano. I'll never understand why they say it like that. I know when I ask
for oregano out there they're like

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:04.380
what? It's like oregano... But you
actually sort of just throw a few drops

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:08.340
down there.
it's disgusting, oh wow, you burn your throat but

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:14.070
I do tend to find it shortens the cold.
Awesome. And are there any beauty

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:17.550
products that you swear by that our
followers should know about? Anything

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:23.580
you've used forever and ever? Well I
absolutely love Sisely products I think

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:31.670
they are beautiful. I love them. And Clé de Peau do the most fantastic little

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:36.960
foundation sticks. I love them. Concealer
sticks I should say. I've got a bit

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:40.800
of a quickfire round. Go on then.
Just either/or. So mascara or

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:43.070
lipstick? *Gasp* Mascara.

00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:53.100
Fish or chips? Oh, um chips. Beach holiday or
a city break? Hmm I quite like a city break

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:59.310
now. 
Madonna or Britney? Oh. Britney. Full glam or

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:07.640
all-natural? All-natural. And horror films
